1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,520
>> Press a key on this machine, and a lamp lights up another letter.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:12,240
>> Press the same key again, and a different lamp lights, and then another.

3
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:19,080
>> In the war, the army's version could be set up in nearly 159 quintillion ways.

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,730
>> 159 quintillion? That sounds unbreakable.

5
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:30,870
>> But it had one odd habit. It never turned a letter into itself.

6
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:39,270
>> That small rule, and the people who saw what it meant, helped break Enigma.

7
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:50,060
>> Enigma has 26 keys and 26 lamps. One operator types; another writes down the lamps that light.

8
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:58,880
>> So let's follow one key press. First, the key turns the right-hand rotor one step.

9
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:04,330
>> Then the current runs to the plugboard, where a cable can swap it for another letter.

10
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:06,800
>> And here, A is plugged to Q.

11
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:12,490
>> Next come three rotors, each wired inside to turn one letter into another.

12
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:18,410
>> At the far end, the reflector sends the current back another way.

13
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:25,290
>> Back through the plugboard, and one lamp lights. Here, A became C.

14
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:33,880
>> Now press A again. The rotor has moved, so every wire has shifted. A becomes N.

15
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:40,300
>> So the same key gives a new letter, because every press uses a fresh scrambled alphabet.

16
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:44,120
>> Do the other two rotors ever move?

17
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:50,940
>> Once per turn, a notch lets the right rotor push the middle one along, like a car's odometer.

18
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:57,110
Almost. The middle rotor sometimes steps twice in a row, which is called the double step.

19
00:01:57,190 --> 00:02:00,160
>> So it's not quite an odometer.

20
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:08,160
>> No. The rotors come back to where they started only after 16,900 key presses.

21
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:19,950
>> The reflector joins the 26 letters in 13 pairs, and turns the current round.

22
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:27,640
>> So the machine works both ways. At the same setting, press C, and the current runs backwards to A.

23
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:36,560
>> Set a second machine the same way, type in the ciphertext, and out comes the message.

24
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:40,650
>> There's no switch between coding and decoding at all.

25
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:45,870
>> But there's a catch. Could a key ever light its own lamp?

26
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:50,660
>> Only if the current came back along the very wire it went in on.

27
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:55,950
That would mean the reflector joining a letter to itself, and it never does.

28
00:02:56,030 --> 00:03:04,350
>> So no letter is ever enciphered as itself. Press A, and you get anything but A.

29
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:10,440
>> We tried every key at every rotor position. Not one lit its own lamp.

30
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,360
>> Couldn't the plugboard fix that?

31
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:19,510
>> No. It swaps a letter on the way in, and swaps it back on the way out.

32
00:03:22,110 --> 00:03:27,930
>> So how many ways could the army's Enigma actually be set up?

33
00:03:28,010 --> 00:03:33,830
>> Let's count. There are three rotors from a box of five, and ten plugboard cables.

34
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:42,870
>> Choosing the rotors and their order gives 5 × 4 × 3, which is 60 ways.

35
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:52,290
>> Their starting positions give 26 × 26 × 26, which is 17,576.

36
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:59,160
And ten cables pairing up letters on the plugboard give about 151 trillion ways.

37
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:11,030
>> Multiply them: nearly 159 quintillion settings, even before the rotors' ring settings.

38
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:16,600
>> At a million settings a second, trying them all would take about five million years.

39
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,470
>> No wonder its users felt safe.

40
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:29,050
>> But nobody broke Enigma by trying every setting. They found shortcuts, and the first came from Poland.

41
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:39,610
In 1929, Poland's Cipher Bureau trained German-speaking mathematics students in Poznań, in secret.

42
00:04:39,690 --> 00:04:43,010
>> Why did they want German speakers?

43
00:04:43,090 --> 00:04:47,080
>> Poznań had been German before the First World War.

44
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:59,330
>> In September 1932, the Bureau took on three of them, Marian Rejewski, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski.

45
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:08,600
>> Rejewski spotted a German habit. Each message began with its own rotor setting, typed twice, from a start shared that day.

46
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:15,620
So the 1st and 4th letters hide the same letter. So do the 2nd and 5th, and the 3rd and 6th.

47
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:21,970
From a day's messages, those links join up into loops, which come in pairs of the same length.

48
00:05:22,050 --> 00:05:30,390
>> And the plugboard changes which letters sit in each loop, but never how long the loops are.

49
00:05:30,470 --> 00:05:35,760
>> So the lengths of the loops point to the rotor settings, whatever the plugboard does.

50
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:43,260
>> A cryptology scholar later called this "the theorem that won World War Two."

51
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:48,080
>> Rejewski still needed the rotors' wiring. Help came from a spy.

52
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:57,450
Hans-Thilo Schmidt, at Germany's Cipher Office, sold secrets to the French, including two months of keys from 1932.

53
00:05:57,530 --> 00:06:02,270
>> And in December, the French shared them with Poland.

54
00:06:02,350 --> 00:06:10,040
>> Another puzzle was the keys' wiring. Rejewski guessed plain order, A to A and B to B, and was right.

55
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:13,810
>> So it was just the alphabet, in order.

56
00:06:13,890 --> 00:06:22,640
>> By the end of 1932, he had all three rotors and the reflector, without ever seeing the machine.

57
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:34,930
From January 1933, the Poles read Enigma. As the Germans tightened their rules, they kept finding new ways in.

58
00:06:35,010 --> 00:06:44,650
>> From September 1938, each operator picked his own start, but still typed the key twice.

59
00:06:44,730 --> 00:06:52,550
>> Sometimes a repeated letter came out the same twice. Zygalski punched sheets with holes wherever that could happen.

60
00:06:52,630 --> 00:07:00,950
>> Stack the sheets for a day's messages, line them up, and fewer and fewer holes shine through.

61
00:07:01,030 --> 00:07:08,420
>> Here are seven, from a simulated day. The one hole left gives away the ring settings.

62
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:11,910
>> And they cut all those holes by hand?

63
00:07:11,990 --> 00:07:15,950
>> Yes, they cut them by hand, with razor blades.

64
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:23,470
Rejewski also designed the bomba, which was in effect six Enigmas wired together and driven by a motor.

65
00:07:23,550 --> 00:07:32,420
>> Six were built, one for each order of the rotors. A day's key took about two hours.

66
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:40,960
>> Then, in December 1938, the Germans added two more rotors. Three from five made 60 orders, not six.

67
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:47,380
Sixty bombas would have cost fifteen times the Bureau's yearly budget for equipment.

68
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:56,650
So in late July 1939, at a secret site outside Warsaw, the Poles showed French and British codebreakers everything.

69
00:07:56,730 --> 00:08:03,770
>> They shared their Enigma copies, the sheets, the bomba and their methods.

70
00:08:03,850 --> 00:08:07,840
>> Five weeks later, Germany invaded Poland.

71
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:12,340
>> They passed it on just in time.

72
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:22,600
>> At Bletchley Park, Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman reported for work the day after Britain declared war.

73
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:32,100
>> Turing expected the Germans to stop typing the key twice. In May 1940, they did.

74
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:38,740
>> So Bletchley leaned on cribs, which were stretches of a message they could guess, like set phrases.

75
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:40,560
>> Such as?

76
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:47,330
>> One outpost in North Africa regularly sent a phrase meaning nothing special to report.

77
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:50,450
>> But where in the message would it be?

78
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:54,900
>> Slide the crib along the ciphertext, one place at a time.

79
00:08:54,980 --> 00:09:03,170
Enigma never enciphers a letter as itself, so wherever a crib letter sits on the same letter, the crib can't be there.

80
00:09:03,250 --> 00:09:10,740
>> Like here, in the second place, where an R sits on an R. That one's crossed out.

81
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:15,020
>> Slide on, and cross out every clash.

82
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:24,560
>> Of these 16 places, 9 are ruled out, without any machine at all.

83
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:30,580
>> The guess says what the message holds. The flaw says where it can't be.

84
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:38,440
>> But a crib in the right place still doesn't give you the key.

85
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:41,510
>> No. For that, Turing designed the bombe.

86
00:09:41,590 --> 00:09:46,480
Line the crib up with the ciphertext, and each pair of letters is a link.

87
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:54,950
>> Draw all the links, and you get a menu. Where links close into loops, it gets powerful.

88
00:09:55,030 --> 00:10:01,970
>> At each rotor position, the bombe guesses one letter's plug partner and follows the consequences.

89
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:10,020
>> And a contradiction means that position is impossible, so the drums turn on.

90
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:17,740
>> Gordon Welchman added the diagonal board, which uses the rule that if A is plugged to B, B is plugged to A.

91
00:10:17,820 --> 00:10:20,860
>> Does that really make a difference?

92
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:28,900
>> We ran our menu through every position. Without the diagonal board, it stopped 662 times.

93
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:36,870
>> With it, it stopped just twice, and the true stop showed E plugged to J.

94
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:43,990
>> The first bombe, Victory, arrived in March 1940, built by Harold Keen's engineers.

95
00:10:44,070 --> 00:10:55,260
>> By 1945 there were about 200, run by some 2,000 Wrens of the Royal Navy.

96
00:10:57,860 --> 00:11:03,900
>> The hardest Enigma was the navy's, with three rotors from eight and stricter procedures.

97
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:11,220
Turing took it on, he said, because no one else was doing anything about it, and he could have it to himself.

98
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:19,120
It also took captured papers. In May 1941, sailors seized codebooks and keys from U-110.

99
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:27,520
>> Through late 1941, decrypts helped steer convoys around the waiting U-boats.

100
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:36,810
Then, on 1 February 1942, the U-boats got a new Enigma with four rotors.

101
00:11:36,890 --> 00:11:43,380
>> For ten months, Bletchley couldn't read them, and the wolf packs ravaged convoys.

102
00:11:43,460 --> 00:11:46,120
>> So how did they get back in?

103
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:56,070
>> In October 1942, off Port Said, sailors from HMS Petard climbed into the sinking U-559 for its papers.

104
00:11:56,150 --> 00:12:04,820
>> Two of them, Tony Fasson and Colin Grazier, were still inside when it sank, and drowned.

105
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:10,170
>> By December 1942, U-boat messages were being read again.

106
00:12:10,250 --> 00:12:14,090
>> So how much did that decide the Atlantic?

107
00:12:14,170 --> 00:12:19,410
>> That's still argued. Radar and much else mattered too.

108
00:12:22,010 --> 00:12:31,380
>> In the film The Imitation Game, Turing builds one machine almost alone, and calls it Christopher.

109
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:37,050
>> In fact, the first bombe was Victory, and breaking Enigma took thousands of people.

110
00:12:37,130 --> 00:12:38,850
>> Thousands?

111
00:12:38,930 --> 00:12:48,490
>> By January 1945, Bletchley Park and its outstations had almost 9,000 staff. About three quarters were women.

112
00:12:48,570 --> 00:12:51,540
>> And what was all that work worth?

113
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:59,940
>> Harry Hinsley, a veteran and later Bletchley's official historian, judged their work shortened the war by at least two years.

114
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:07,780
>> Others find any such number too precise. No one can run a war twice.

115
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:18,170
>> One of the last messages Bletchley decoded was sent from Cuxhaven on 7 May 1945. British troops had entered the town.

116
00:13:18,250 --> 00:13:27,470
>> And the operator signed off: "Closing down forever. All the best. Goodbye."

117
00:13:28,270 --> 00:13:34,510
>> The reflector made Enigma convenient. It also meant no letter could ever be itself.

118
00:13:34,590 --> 00:13:38,410
>> On its own, that flaw wasn't enough.

119
00:13:38,490 --> 00:13:45,010
>> No. But add German habits, captured papers and thousands of patient people, and it was.

120
00:13:45,090 --> 00:13:52,010
>> Press A, and you could get any letter at all, except A.
